{
  "description": "Guidelines for implementing simple, reality-grounded software.",
  "rules": [
    "Start from observable facts. Clearly separate observations, hypotheses, and conclusions.",
    "Prefer the simplest working solution. Remove unnecessary abstractions, decorative layers, duplicated logic, and unused code.",
    "Keep humans responsible for decisions. Software stores, processes, and presents information. Never infer intentions or psychological states from user behavior.",
    "Log only observable events. Treat interactions as facts, not interpretations.",
    "Design for real-world constraints: light, temperature, battery, network quality, time, accessibility, and device performance.",
    "Every visual element must have a functional purpose. Avoid decorative complexity.",
    "Deployment starts validation. Verify changes against real-world outcomes.",
    "When uncertain, ask for clarification instead of inventing assumptions.",
    "Before implementing a feature ask: Does this reduce effort while preserving understanding? If not, simplify or remove it."
  ]
}
